WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.383
I went to Cedric's late one afternoon expecting the unexpected and sure enough I got

00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:09.880
the unexpected.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.760
It was an empty office, nobody else was there but Cedric.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.880
No hint that it was an architect's office at all.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:24.480
It was a black and grey room, no architectural models, no drawings lying around.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.100
There was what looked as if it could have been a drawing board with a black cover on

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.160
it giving nothing away at all.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:40.682
And Cedric was there very much with his big presence, a looming figure, somebody

00:00:40.682 --> 00:00:41.340
looking

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:48.210
quite important, very sure of himself, charming man, very friendly, put me at ease

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:48.880
straight

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:49.880
away.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:58.820
sat down, went through the work I'd brought along and he offered me a job

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.280
there and then, which was great. When he offered me the job I said, wow thank you

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.400
very much, yes please, I'll take it straight away. It was like an

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.660
unbelievable opportunity, I'd always wanted to work for him but never thought

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.780
it was worth writing in out of the blue because he must be inundated with

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:25.040
inquiries and he made me a fair financial offer which quite surprised me.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.640
I was really keen to work for him I'd always idolized what I'd seen of his

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.400
work published in the various magazines and books and he was one of the

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.780
inspirational figures that I just wanted to work for. Cedric was very much a

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:54.460
bon viveur. He looked like he enjoyed life and I think he did. He often had his

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:59.720
cigar, his trademark cigar. He liked his brandy very much as well, often drinking

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.900
it a bit too early in the morning but that was Cedric. He was very warm, very

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.960
generous. I never saw him lose his temper. For an architect he was actually very

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:19.836
very calm. I never saw Cedric really being sad at all. He was always full of

00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:21.080
anticipation

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.927
of the future. Never really looked back, always looked ahead. Great belief in what

00:02:26.927 --> 00:02:27.640
the future

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:34.120
would bring. I think he had his frustrations because he hadn't built much and I

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:34.940
think that

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:40.920
was very sad. I think he really wanted to build. Everything that he did and the way

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.561
he did it led me to believe he did want to build. The projects were very thoroughly

00:02:45.561 --> 00:02:46.120
researched

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.177
from the outset, a lot of site analysis, a lot of brief analysis, a lot of

00:02:51.177 --> 00:02:52.680
challenging the brief,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:59.226
an awful lot of studies as we can see through the archives on all of the projects,

00:02:59.226 --> 00:02:59.800
everything

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.436
explored in the minutest detail. I don't believe he'd have done that had he not

00:03:05.436 --> 00:03:06.120
thought the

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.220
the projects would go ahead.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.400
But sadly, and it's not unusual, it happens to every architect,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.200
an awful lot of the projects they work on never go ahead.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.580
I was there with Cedric for two years,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.760
and during that time we worked on a wide range of projects.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.660
The sort of biggest was South Bank's study for GLC.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.760
There was the Parc de Villette competition in Paris,

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.440
which he didn't win, which Bernard Schumi won.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:38.760
there was going down the scale Battersea Power Station which I suspect he never

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.880
really thought

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:48.178
he would win but it was a fantastic proposal that he made. A small aircraft hangar

00:03:48.178 --> 00:03:48.380
BA

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:56.356
there was a housing scheme in Australia for Alistair McAlpine and the Cedric Price

00:03:56.356 --> 00:03:56.940
Aviary

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:03.520
they were the main ones. Battersea Power Station or BATHAT as he called the

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:10.080
competition was was a building that's that's been lying dormant for years it

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.400
has like lay dormant for many years before the competition the competition

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.780
was was launched I think Cedric thought nothing would happen for for a good many

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:23.580
years and in the end he was proved right I think he felt that what was important

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.560
the building was the view on the skyline, the chimneys and the part of the

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.100
structure immediately beneath that, so he left that and basically took the rest

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:40.380
out. It was a cover for creating potential for future users and so on at

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.000
that time undefined and I think he was proved right, you know the project, the

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.140
site lay dormant for the next 30 years, it's still empty, it's about to be

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.480
developed but it took a lot of boom and busts and developers going bankrupt

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:58.160
before anything is now finally happening and it's only now happening thanks to

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:04.160
the London boom and the absurdly high property prices in London that enable

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.160
these more difficult sites to finally be developed and it's a shame it's for a

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:14.700
largely housing based scheme rather than public uses. When I joined the office I

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.480
was the only architect working for Cedric there was there was a secretary

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:25.020
me and Cedric. So I was the assistant and when a project came in whether it was a

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:30.900
new project or a competition I would go about doing the analysis, visiting the

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:37.600
site, doing the site survey, all the things an architect does. We'd then sit

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.460
down and go through the competition brief, go through all the data I'd got

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.280
and so on. And generally what would happen is Cedric would then go away and

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:53.356
think. Office hours were 8 to 3. We arrived at 8 in the morning, rolled up the

00:05:53.356 --> 00:05:53.900
silver

00:05:53.900 --> 00:06:00.134
roll of lines, made some coffee, Cedric would come in soon after. Often I'd find on

00:06:00.134 --> 00:06:00.660
my desk

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.489
a pile of drawings he'd done the night before and that's often how he worked. So he

00:06:05.489 --> 00:06:05.780
would

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:11.837
work in his own quiet time, put a pile of drawings on the desk and then at some

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:12.220
point

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:19.130
during the day we'd go through them and I'd then develop the drawings and then we'd

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:19.380
sit

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:27.249
down again another day, go through it, chat, he'd have a think, respond and so it

00:06:27.249 --> 00:06:28.220
continued.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:35.737
The White Room was very much Cedric's domain, it's where he entertained his

00:06:35.737 --> 00:06:36.940
visitors. In

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:42.365
In the morning he would come in, he'd do the post, then make a number of phone

00:06:42.365 --> 00:06:43.060
calls, and

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:46.220
often during the day visitors would come in and out.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:51.626
Raina Bannam was a frequent visitor, Joan Littlewood, Monica Pidgeon, Paul Finch,

00:06:51.626 --> 00:06:52.160
Alistair

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.300
McAlpine, a long, long list of his close friends and contacts.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.540
He'd always take them upstairs.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:06.580
Alistair would often come round for breakfast, always have a bacon butty.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.580
We generally didn't go up to the White Room.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.860
We might go up to the White Room if Cedric had been working up there

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:15.160
and there was a lot of sketches to come, to bring down.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.720
But it was his domain, his private world.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.660
He kept his world separate.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.860
There was the world of his office.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.820
There was his close friends, Alford, Henderson, Frank Newby.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.240
And then there was the world of the other architects, the AA.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.040
And they were all very much pigeonholed and kept separate.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:43.276
I'm not sure why Cedric kept those three different realms but they did come

00:07:43.276 --> 00:07:44.080
together.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.588
Once a year at Christmas he threw a very lavish Christmas party and people from the

00:07:49.588 --> 00:07:49.920
three

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:56.655
different worlds were brought together and at election time, general election time,

00:07:56.655 --> 00:07:56.980
he'd

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:02.460
throw an election party and then there again the three worlds might come together.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.760
Otherwise they were kept separate.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:11.298
The South Bank project was commissioned by Tony Banks, who was head of culture at

00:08:11.298 --> 00:08:11.540
the

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:19.716
Greater London Council and one of Cedric's close friends. The South Bank was in a

00:08:19.716 --> 00:08:20.120
very

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:26.745
sad state. It's an area the size of Covent Garden and Soho put together. It

00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:27.540
contained

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:32.206
County Hall, which was the headquarters of the Greater London Council. It contained

00:08:32.206 --> 00:08:32.600
Shell's

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:39.920
headquarters, National Theatre, Royal Festival Hall, the Film Museum.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.714
The public realm between them was sort of empty, abandoned, unloved, dangerous at

00:08:46.714 --> 00:08:47.140
night.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.880
And Tony Banks was keen for Cedric to sort of cast his eye over it

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:57.860
and come up with a master plan for enhancing that key site in the heart of London.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.740
So it was quite a long process, an awful lot of analysis,

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:08.023
many, many meetings. Cedric attended meeting after meeting after meeting with all

00:09:08.023 --> 00:09:08.760
the various

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:16.588
interested parties. And after about six, nine months, we came up with the proposals

00:09:16.588 --> 00:09:17.060
which

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:23.743
I think were good urban design proposals about networks of public routes, uses for

00:09:23.743 --> 00:09:24.640
the spaces,

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:31.971
hierarchies and I guess the headline grabber was a giant ferris wheel next to County

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:32.320
Hall

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.520
which ultimately was built by another architect. The project

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:48.903
took the form of drawings, reports, number of documents, presentations and it was

00:09:48.903 --> 00:09:49.840
handed over

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:57.407
to the GLC and nothing really came of it in the end. It wasn't the first project by

00:09:57.407 --> 00:09:57.680
the

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.898
GLC that nothing came of for that site, there'd been project after project after

00:10:02.898 --> 00:10:03.420
project.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:09.545
I think it was so politically charged that it was going to be one of those projects

00:10:09.545 --> 00:10:09.840
that

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:15.186
was going to be very difficult to see the light of day. And in the end it sort of

00:10:15.186 --> 00:10:15.780
happened,

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.345
improvements have happened piecemeal but there's never, none of the projects looking

00:10:19.345 --> 00:10:19.600
at the

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:26.000
site-wide have ever happened. For me, one of the highlights was the office parties.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:35.179
When we got a glimpse into Cedric's other worlds, the General Election Party in

00:10:35.179 --> 00:10:36.260
particular

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:42.030
were amazing, amazing things. His political friends would drop in and out, his AA

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:42.600
friends,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:49.020
Eleanor, the people from the office, ex-members of staff. Cedric's brother would be

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:49.360
there

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:52.800
with a thing called a penguinometer, which was a swingometer,

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.340
which would swing according to whether Labour or the Conservatives were in power.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:04.180
I used BBC data and programmed a Sinclair ZX81 computer

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.220
to make election projections.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:12.740
The year I was there was when Margaret Thatcher was re-elected for a second time.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:18.220
So given Cedric and his friends' more socialist tendencies,

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.760
it ended up not being a very jolly occasion,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.820
but it was very interesting to get an insight into Cedric's life.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.840
Cedric's one of these people that I've never seen being really vulnerable.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:32.060
He's always been completely in control of the situation.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:37.460
I think Cedric's stature helped.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.360
His incredible intellect helped.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.120
I think it was very difficult for people to attack him.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:53.460
He was obviously a very controversial figure, enjoyed winding people up, enjoyed

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.160
coming

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.509
out with extraordinary statements sometimes like perhaps York Minster should be

00:11:58.509 --> 00:11:59.060
demolished

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:04.404
or I think it was Cedric who once told the clients I've been through all the data

00:12:04.404 --> 00:12:04.800
you've

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.920
given me and I don't think a new building will help your problem.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.070
Cedric always talked about the role of the architect being to enable rather than to

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:14.320
build

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:22.263
and there are probably a number of interpretations of that. For me the enabler is

00:12:22.263 --> 00:12:23.440
possibly can

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:29.686
be explained by Fun Palace where the only permanent part of the building of Fun

00:12:29.686 --> 00:12:30.160
Palace

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:36.129
was really the structure and arguably the crane. So it wasn't really a building at

00:12:36.129 --> 00:12:36.420
all.

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:43.340
parts of the building committed a huge range of activities to be supported

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:50.640
through the use of projection screens, through the use of demountable elements,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:58.360
through the use of cabins and that in its totality created what was the Fun

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:03.420
Palace with its various concert halls, its exhibitions, its screens and so on.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:08.180
but the building itself was just the structure and the crane.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:15.865
I guess I'm not the only one to say that the favourite Cedric project was Fun

00:13:15.865 --> 00:13:16.480
Palace.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:23.520
I think it was extraordinary the impact it's had on the architectural profession

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:31.760
and what was considered quite amazing at that time is now standard practice

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:36.534
or became standard practice, the use of a structural framework rather than

00:13:36.534 --> 00:13:37.760
load-bearing walls,

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:43.771
the incredible use of technology, the use of prefabricated elements, the use of

00:13:43.771 --> 00:13:44.760
kitted parts,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:51.760
demountable elements, the large projection screens which didn't exist then,

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.760
the sort of foresight he had was quite incredible.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:14:00.487
And all those elements came to be standard practice for the generation that followed

00:14:00.487 --> 00:14:00.760
him,

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:07.180
him, the Rogers, Foster, Sterling, Grimshaw and currently used by all the spin-offs

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:15.380
from those four practices. I think you can see traces of Cedric's

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:26.320
philosophy and approach in many of the projects of Foster, Rogers, Grimshaw,

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:33.940
Hopkins and of all the various spin-off practices that have come from those four

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:42.040
main practices over the years. You know obviously Pompidou Center in a watered

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:48.260
down version of Fun Palace, obviously in the whole kit of parts approach used by

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:55.120
Sterling, by Foster Rogers and Grimshaw. I think Cedric taught us all to think

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:03.280
laterally and the importance of that of looking beyond the box not not being

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:07.660
pigeonholed and possibly the way we're all we've all been trained at many

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:11.120
schools of architecture around the world

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:20.520
he taught us to unleash possibilities not to take things for granted to an

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:27.400
architecture should be a way of enabling things to happen often in an

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:34.520
undetermined way. He certainly taught us that architecture isn't all about

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:39.740
stylistic interventions and trophy architecture and unfortunately I think

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:46.460
there's been too much of that with the rise of the superstar architect. There's

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:52.535
there's been less of the, far too little importance placed on the lessons we've all

00:15:52.535 --> 00:15:53.340
learnt from

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:59.460
Cedric but they're still all relevant and fortunately still all practised.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:05.360
For me the most important question to ask about Cedric Price is why didn't he build

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:12.920
more? I think there's a common conception that he was quite happy not building but I

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:19.200
I think he really wanted to build and he'd have liked to have built a lot more.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:24.520
He was a very good communicator, very good indeed.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:28.520
His projects were widely published in the press and so on.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:34.848
He didn't have a PR department, he didn't have press people handling it, he did it

00:16:34.848 --> 00:16:35.080
all

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:36.080
himself.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:41.248
He was very hands on as he was in every aspect of the practice and his work was

00:16:41.248 --> 00:16:42.360
widely published.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:53.160
and I think he became very influential through all of that and I think many believed

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:55.860
his ideas were right,

00:16:55.860 --> 00:17:02.860
they were enthusiastic about his ideas, adopted them, built them,

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:07.450
so I think Cedric was probably very happy to see his ideas come to fruition, but

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:09.860
very disappointed he didn't do it himself.
